    "message": "Let natural-language interventions reach Opus and say why they fell back\n\nA generated Scenario's intervention compiler context was larger than the\nrouter's generic 16,384-byte input guard at every population: the installed\nprimitive descriptions alone are about 13 KB. The browser preview therefore\nnever called the API and silently used the offline rules.\n\nLLMTask can now declare its own input byte limit. The router uses the larger\nof that and the setting, and reserves the budget check against it. The limit\nis not part of the cache key, so recorded tapes keep their keys. The compiler\ndeclares 64 KiB, enough for roughly 1,500 people.\n\nEvery rule-fallback compiler result now names, in fixed Japanese text with a\nreason code, why Opus was not used (offline mode, input limit, call cap,\nbudget, 429, invalid output and so on).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
